{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VYsZaXV1cfTdBqaUatSs17","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L2/080 MoE complete layer with shared expert (backward)\n2\t\n3\tRun ID: `formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-080_moe_complete_layer_with_shared_expert_backward`\n4\tTarget GPU: **NVIDIA A800, `sm_80` (Ampere)**. Triton is the required compute path.\n5\t\n6\t> Status: analysis only. No `plan.md` and no solution code are written in this step.\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## 1. Task contract and entry point\n11\t\n12\tWe must expose `solution/solution.py::run(...)` with the exact signature and output\n13\tordering of the reference in `task/definition.json`. All *computation* must be Triton;\n14\tPyTorch may only be used for tensor metadata, allocation, dtype/stride bookkeeping, and\n15\tkernel launch plumbing. **No** Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback is\n16\tpermitted — a failing Triton path is invalid, not something to substitute.\n17\t\n18\tConstant axes (fixed across all workloads):\n19\t\n20\t| symbol | meaning | value |\n21\t|--------|---------|-------|\n22\t| `H`  | `hidden_size`            | 4096 |\n23\t| `I`  | `moe_intermediate_size`  | 1408 |\n24\t| `E`  | `n_routed_experts`       | 128  |\n25\t| `K`  | `num_experts_per_tok`    | 8    |\n26\t| `T`  | `batch_seq_len` (variable) | 211, 293, 384, 512, 997 (feedback set) |\n27\t\n28\tScalars baked into the reference: `routed_scaling_factor = 1.0`, `norm_topk_prob = True`,\n29\t`n_group = topk_group = 1` (so `score_mask` is all ones), `eps = 1e-20`.\n30\t\n31\t### Inputs (dtype / shape)\n32\t- `grad_output`  bf16 `[T,H]`\n33\t- `hidden_states`  bf16 `[T,H]`\n34\t- `router_weight`  bf16 `[E,H]`\n35\t- `e_score_correction_bias`  f32 `[E]`  (all zeros in the generator, but not assumed)\n36\t- `router_logits`  f32 `[T,E]`  (unused by the reference `run`)\n37\t- `scores`  f32 `[T,E]`  (sigmoid outputs)\n38\t- `topk_indices`  int64 `[T,K]`\n39\t- `topk_weights`  f32 `[T,K]`  (already normalized to sum≈1 per row in the generator)\n40\t- `score_mask`  f32 `[T,E]`  (all ones here)\n41\t- `shared_expert_gate_weight`  bf16 `[I,H]`\n42\t- `shared_expert_up_weight`  bf16 `[I,H]`\n43\t- `shared_expert_down_weight`  bf16 `[H,I]`\n44\t- `shared_gate_output`  bf16 `[T,I]`\n45\t- `shared_up_output`  bf16 `[T,I]`\n46\t- `shared_activated`  bf16 `[T,I]`\n47\t\n48\t### Outputs (ordered tuple)\n49\t1. `grad_hidden_states`  bf16 `[T,H]`\n50\t2. `grad_router_weight`  **f32** `[E,H]`\n51\t3. `grad_shared_expert_gate_weight`  bf16 `[I,H]`\n52\t4. `grad_shared_expert_up_weight`  bf16 `[I,H]`\n53\t5. `grad_shared_expert_down_weight`  bf16 `[H,I]`\n54\t\n55\tNote the reference `run` is decorated `@torch.no_grad()` — it is a hand-written backward,\n56\tnot autograd. We reproduce its arithmetic exactly (op-for-op semantics), not \"the correct\n57\tmath\"; the reference contains an intentional *approximation* for the routed-expert path\n58\t(see §4.3) that we must match, not fix.\n59\t\n60\t---\n61\t\n62\t## 2. Exact decomposition of the reference computation\n63\t\n64\tTwo independent paths that both accumulate into `grad_hidden_states`.\n65\t\n66\t### 2.1 Shared-expert path (numerically dominant)\n67\t\n68\tLet `go = grad_output` (`grad_shared_output` is just a clone of it).\n69\t\n70\t- **G1** `grad_shared_activated = go @ down_w`  → `[T,I]`.\n71\t  `down_w` is `[H,I]`; bf16·bf16, fp32 accumulate, output bf16. Contraction `K=H=4096`.\n72\t- **G2** `grad_shared_expert_down_weight = (go.T.f32) @ (shared_activated.f32)` → `[H,I]`,\n73\t  cast to bf16. Contraction `K=T` (small). **Output #5.**\n74\t- **SwiGLU backward** (elementwise, `[T,I]`):\n75\t  - `grad_gate_silu = grad_shared_activated * shared_up_output`\n76\t  - `grad_up_output = grad_shared_activated * silu(shared_gate_output)`   ← `grad_shared_up_output`\n77\t  - `sig = sigmoid(gate.f32)`; `silu'(x) = sig·(1 + x·(1−sig))`\n78\t  - `grad_gate_output = (grad_gate_silu.f32) · silu'(gate)` → bf16   ← `grad_shared_gate_output`\n79\t- **G3** `grad_hidden_from_shared_up = grad_up_output @ up_w`  → `[T,H]` bf16. `up_w=[I,H]`, `K=I`.\n80\t- **G4** `grad_hidden_from_shared_gate = grad_gate_output @ gate_w` → `[T,H]` bf16. `K=I`.\n81\t- **G5** `grad_shared_expert_up_weight = (grad_up_output.T.f32) @ (hidden.f32)` → `[I,H]`→bf16.\n82\t  `K=T`. **Output #4.**\n83\t- **G6** `grad_shared_expert_gate_weight = (grad_gate_output.T.f32) @ (hidden.f32)` → `[I,H]`→bf16.\n84\t  `K=T`. **Output #3.**\n85\t- `grad_hidden_states = grad_hidden_from_shared_up + grad_hidden_from_shared_gate`.\n86\t\n87\t### 2.2 Router path (numerically ~zero, but must be produced)\n88\t\n89\t- `grad_norm_sq = rowsum(go.f32²)` → `[T,1]`; `grad_topk_weights = grad_norm_sq / K` broadcast to `[T,K]`.\n90\t  (This is the *isotropic straight-through* approximation; see §4.3.)\n91\t- Normalization-Jacobian (quotient rule), with `routed_scaling_factor=1`:\n92\t  - `S = rowsum(topk_weights) + 1e-20`\n93\t  - `sum_grad = rowsum(grad_topk_weights · topk_weights) / S`\n94\t  - `grad_topk_before_norm = (grad_topk_weights − sum_grad) / S`   → `[T,K]`\n95\t- **Scatter** `grad_scores[T,E] = 0; scatter_add_(dim=1, topk_indices, grad_topk_before_norm)`.\n96\t- `grad_scores *= score_mask` (all ones ⇒ no-op, but keep general).\n97\t- `grad_router_logits = grad_scores · scores · (1 − scores)` → `[T,E]` f32 (sigmoid derivative).\n98\t- **G7** `grad_hidden_from_router = grad_router_logits.to(bf16) @ router_w` → `[T,H]` bf16. `K=E=128`.\n99\t- **G8** `grad_router_weight = grad_router_logits.T @ hidden.f32` → `[E,H]` **f32**. `K=T`. **Output #2.**\n100\t- `grad_hidden_states += grad_hidden_from_router`.\n101\t\n102\t---\n103\t\n104\t## 3. Compute / cost analysis\n105\t\n106\tEight GEMMs plus a handful of elementwise/reduction/scatter kernels. Dominant FLOPs are the\n107\tsix shared-path GEMMs (each ≈ `2·T·H·I`, with `H·I ≈ 5.77M`); router GEMMs use `E=128`\n108\tinstead of `I=1408` (≈11× cheaper) and the scatter is trivial.\n109\t\n110\tTwo structural families of GEMM:\n111\t- **\"Tall/skinny output\" GEMMs** with contraction `K=H` or `K=I` (G1, G3, G4, G7): output has\n112\t  `T` rows (211–997) → short-M GEMMs.\n113\t- **\"Outer-product\" weight-grad GEMMs** with contraction `K=T` (small): G2 `[H,I]`, G5/G6 `[I,H]`,\n114\t  G8 `[E,H]`. Small contraction, large outputs (`[H,I]` bf16 ≈ 11.5 MB each). These are\n115\t  latency/memory sensitive, not FLOP-bound.\n116\t\n117\tBecause `T` is small, every GEMM here is a *small* GEMM where kernel-launch overhead and\n118\tmemory round-trips dominate. That is precisely the regime where a **fused Triton**\n119\timplementation can beat the reference's ~8 cuBLAS calls + ~6 elementwise kernels + a\n120\tscatter, even if a single Triton GEMM is not faster than cuBLAS in isolation. The win comes\n121\tfrom *fewer launches* and *fewer materialized intermediates*, not from out-GEMMing cuBLAS.\n122\t\n123\t### 3.1 Fusion opportunities (the core of the speedup thesis)\n124\t\n125\t- **Epilogue-fuse SwiGLU backward into G1**: G1 produces `grad_shared_activated[T,I]`; the\n126\t  SwiGLU-backward elementwise turns it (with `shared_up_output`, `shared_gate_output`) into\n127\t  `grad_up_output` and `grad_gate_output`. Fusing this into G1's epilogue avoids writing\n128\t  `grad_shared_activated` and a separate elementwise pass over `[T,I]`.\n129\t- **Concatenate the grad_hidden GEMMs G3+G4+G7** into one GEMM:\n130\t  `grad_hidden = [grad_up_output | grad_gate_output | grad_router_logits.bf16] @ [up_w; gate_w; router_w]`\n131\t  with contraction `K = I + I + E = 2944`. One `[T,H]` output, one accumulator, replaces three\n132\t  GEMMs and two adds. (All three left operands are bf16, all three right operands bf16 ⇒ clean.)\n133\t- **Concatenate the shared weight-grad GEMMs G5+G6** into one GEMM\n134\t  `[grad_up_output | grad_gate_output].T @ hidden → [2I, H]` (contraction `K=T`), splitting the\n135\t  `[2I,H]` output into the two `[I,H]` bf16 results. G2 is a separate `[H,I]` output (different\n136\t  left operand `go`, different N-operand `shared_activated`) but shares `K=T`.\n137\t- **Router path** (G8 f32, scatter, sigmoid-deriv, normalization) is cheap and numerically\n138\t  delicate; keep it as small dedicated kernels rather than forcing it into the fused bf16 GEMMs.\n139\t\n140\tThese are *plan-stage* choices; the draft only enumerates the design space.\n141\t\n142\t---\n143\t\n144\t## 4. Numerical analysis and risks\n145\t\n146\t### 4.1 Magnitudes (from the generator's distributions)\n147\t- `grad_output ~ N(0,1)`, `H=4096` ⇒ per-row `grad_norm_sq ≈ 4096`; `grad_topk_weights ≈ 512`.\n148\t- Weights scaled by `0.02`; `shared_gate_output/up ~ std≈1.3`; `silu(gate) ~ O(0.5)`.\n149\t- `grad_shared_activated ~ std≈1.3`; `grad_up_output/grad_gate_output ~ O(0.6)`.\n150\t- `grad_hidden_states ~ O(0.4–0.6)` (dominated by the shared up+gate contributions).\n151\t- Shared weight-grads `[I,H]/[H,I]`: contraction `K=T` of `O(0.6)·O(1)` terms ⇒ `O(√T · 0.6)`,\n152\t  i.e. tens; cast to bf16.\n153\t\n154\t### 4.2 dtype-matching (must reproduce reference casts)\n155\t- G1, G3, G4, G7 are bf16·bf16 → bf16 in the reference. Triton `tl.dot` on bf16 inputs with an\n156\t  fp32 accumulator reproduces this (tensor-core computes exact bf16 products, fp32 accumulate),\n157\t  then we round to bf16 on store. **Match risk: low.**\n158\t- G2, G5, G6 upcast bf16 operands to f32 then matmul, then cast the *output* to bf16. Since\n159\t  bf16→f32 is exact, an fp32-accumulate tensor-core GEMM of the bf16 operands yields the same\n160\t  products; the only question is the accumulator/rounding. Given the bf16 output cast, TF32 or\n161\t  bf16-input fp32-accumulate is more than sufficient. **Match risk: low.**\n162\t- G8 keeps f32 output (`grad_router_weight`). Its left operand `grad_router_logits` is genuine\n163\t  f32 (not bf16). But it is numerically ~0 (see §4.3), so precision of this GEMM is immaterial\n164\t  to passing tolerance; still, prefer an fp32 (TF32-accumulate acceptable) path here.\n165\t- `silu`, `sigmoid`, and the SiLU derivative are computed in f32 in the reference before the\n166\t  bf16 cast; do the same in-kernel (compute in fp32, store bf16).\n167\t\n168\t### 4.3 The router-path cancellation (key insight)\n169\tBecause `grad_topk_weights` is *isotropic* (identical value `g` across the `K` slots) and the\n170\tnormalization Jacobian removes the common-mode component:\n171\t`grad_before[j] = (g − g·S/S)/S ≈ 0`. With `topk_weights` already summing to ≈1 and `eps=1e-20`,\n172\t`grad_topk_before_norm ≈ 0` up to fp rounding. Consequently `grad_router_logits ≈ 0`,\n173\t`grad_router_weight ≈ 0`, and `grad_hidden_from_router ≈ 0`.\n174\t\n175\tImplications:\n176\t- `grad_router_weight` (output #2) is a near-zero tensor; comparisons are effectively `atol`-only,\n177\t  which is very forgiving. We must still *produce* it via the same formula (a tiny residual), but\n178\t  precision is a non-issue.\n179\t- The **entire measured error budget lives in the shared-expert path**. Optimization aggressiveness\n180\t  (TF32 vs bf16 accumulate, tiling, split-K) is bounded by the shared-path outputs, not the router.\n181\t- Risk: if we naively \"simplify\" the router path to exactly 0, the residual might differ from the\n182\t  reference by ~1e-4, still far inside `atol`. We will nonetheless reproduce the formula faithfully\n183\t  to avoid surprises and to be robust if a non-zero `e_score_correction_bias` ever appears.\n184\t\n185\t### 4.4 Tolerance interpretation\n186\tFeedback tolerances: `max_atol ∈ {0.12, 0.13, 0.18, 0.2, 0.36}`, `max_rtol = 0.05`,\n187\t`required_match_ratio = 0.98`. Assumed check: element passes if `|a−b| ≤ atol + rtol·|b|`, and\n188\t≥98% of elements per tensor must pass. Given output magnitudes `O(0.5)` and these atols, bf16\n189\trounding (~2⁻⁸ relative ≈ 0.4%) is comfortably inside budget. This confirms we may use bf16\n190\ttensor cores / TF32 freely on the shared path. The 2% \"free\" fraction also absorbs occasional\n191\tlarge-relative-error elements near zero.\n192\t\n193\t### 4.5 Other correctness hazards\n194\t- **`int64` topk_indices**: scatter target indices; must gather/scatter with int64 or safely cast\n195\t  to int32 (values < 128, safe). Triton scatter via `tl.atomic_add` or a per-token serial loop over\n196\t  `K=8`; must guard bounds and match `scatter_add_` accumulation order semantics (add is commutative\n197\t  so order does not affect result, only fp associativity — negligible here since values ~0).\n198\t- **Transposes / strides**: G2/G5/G6/G8 use logical transposes of the left operand. Handle via\n199\t  stride arithmetic in the kernel (do not physically transpose). Weight tensors have specific\n200\t  row/col-major layouts (`down_w`=[H,I], `up_w`/`gate_w`=[I,H]); tile pointers must respect these.\n201\t- **Non-multiple `T`**: `T ∈ {211,293,384,512,997}` — 211, 293, 997 are not multiples of typical\n202\t  block sizes; masking on the `T` dimension (both as M-dim and as K-dim in the outer-product GEMMs)\n203\t  is mandatory. Masked K-loop tails must contribute zero.\n204\t- **Accumulator init / additive outputs**: `grad_hidden_states` is a sum of contributions; if built\n205\t  from a single fused concatenated GEMM the accumulator handles it, otherwise use one zero-init plus\n206\t  in-kernel adds (avoid read-modify-write races across programs).\n207\t- **Autotune / immutability**: Triton autotuning must be deterministic and self-contained within a\n208\t  candidate; any config change is a new candidate ID. Prefer a small fixed config set keyed on `T`\n209\t  buckets to avoid nondeterministic tuning across evaluations.\n210\t\n211\t---\n212\t\n213\t## 5. Constraints checklist\n214\t- Triton-only compute; torch for allocation/metadata/launch only. No fallback of any kind.\n215\t- Immutable sequential candidates `c001, c002, …`; new ID for any meaningful source/config/launch change.\n216\t- Evaluate exclusively via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`; five fixed workloads = one evaluation.\n217\t- Never run `final` without operator approval. Do not touch evaluator/dataset/controller/launcher/config.\n218\t- Budget: ≤100 candidate evaluations; token soft/normal/absolute = 1.0M / 1.5M / 1.65M.\n219\t- Do not run CUDA/profiler/`nvidia-smi`/the raw evaluator directly.\n220\t\n221\t---\n222\t\n223\t## 6. Triton design space\n224\t\n225\t### 6.1 Kernel decomposition options (from conservative → aggressive)\n226\t- **D0 (baseline correctness)**: one Triton kernel per reference op — 8 GEMM kernels + separate\n227\t  elementwise (SwiGLU-bwd, sigmoid-deriv), a row-reduction for `grad_norm_sq`, a scatter kernel,\n228\t  and the normalization. Maximizes correctness confidence; minimal fusion. Establishes that a pure\n229\t  Triton path passes and gives a speed reference vs. torch.\n230\t- **D1 (epilogue fusion)**: fuse SwiGLU backward into G1's epilogue; fuse the sigmoid-derivative and\n231\t  normalization into small elementwise kernels; fuse the three grad_hidden GEMMs into one\n232\t  concatenated-K GEMM (G3+G4+G7); fuse G5+G6 into one concatenated-output GEMM.\n233\t- **D2 (aggressive)**: additionally fuse the `grad_norm_sq` row-reduction + normalization + scatter +\n234\t  sigmoid-deriv into a single router prologue; consider split-K for the `K=T` outer-product GEMMs\n235\t  (G2/G5/G6) when `T` is small to keep SMs busy; persistent-kernel / grouped launch to cut overhead.\n236\t\n237\t### 6.2 GEMM tiling and precision\n238\t- Short-M GEMMs (G1/G3/G4/G7): block `[BM, BN]` with `BM` covering the small `T` (e.g. 64/128), `BN`\n239\t  along `H` or `I`; K-loop over `H`/`I`/`2944`. bf16 inputs, fp32 accumulate.\n240\t- Outer-product GEMMs (G2/G5/G6/G8): contraction `K=T` (small, 211–997). Large outputs; tile\n241\t  `[BM,BN]` over the output; single (or few) K-tiles. Consider split-K + atomic/second-pass reduce for\n242\t  small `T` to improve occupancy. bf16 in / fp32 accum for G2/G5/G6; f32/TF32 for G8.\n243\t- Precision knobs: `tl.dot(..., input_precision=\"tf32\"|\"ieee\")` and bf16 operands. Default plan: bf16\n244\t  tensor cores with fp32 accumulate on the shared path (matches reference, fast on Ampere); reserve\n245\t  IEEE/TF32 only where a candidate shows a match-ratio regression.\n246\t\n247\t### 6.3 Autotuning strategy\n248\t- Small, fixed candidate-local config lists (block sizes, `num_warps`, `num_stages`) selected by a `T`\n249\t  bucket, chosen to be deterministic. Ampere `num_stages` typically 3–4; `num_warps` 4–8. Avoid broad\n250\t  autotune sweeps that could vary across evaluations or blow the token/eval budget.\n251\t\n252\t### 6.4 Launch/plumbing\n253\t- Preallocate all five outputs with `torch.empty`/`zeros` (zeros only where an additive accumulation\n254\t  requires it). Pass strides explicitly. Concatenated-K GEMMs read multiple source tensors via\n255\t  separate base pointers within one kernel rather than a physical `torch.cat` (avoid extra copies),\n256\t  unless a cheap `cat` is measured to be faster.\n257\t\n258\t---\n259\t\n260\t## 7. Candidate roadmap (sketch, to be formalized in plan.md)\n261\t1. **c001** — D0 straightforward per-op Triton (correctness anchor + baseline speed).\n262\t2. **c002** — D1 epilogue-fuse SwiGLU backward into G1; keep other GEMMs separate.\n263\t3. **c003** — D1 concatenated grad_hidden GEMM (G3+G4+G7) and fused G5+G6.\n264\t4. **c004+** — D2 tuning: split-K for `K=T` GEMMs, block/warp/stage tuning per `T` bucket, router\n265\t   prologue fusion. Each measured; keep only non-regressing, correctness-passing steps.\n266\tAdvance only when the previous candidate passes all five workloads.\n267\t\n268\t---\n269\t\n270\t## 8. Validation strategy\n271\t- **Correctness gate**: every candidate must pass all five feedback workloads (≥98% element match at\n272\t  the per-workload tolerance) before its speed is considered. A candidate that fails any workload is\n273\t  invalid and is not promoted.\n274\t- **Numerical self-checks before evaluating** (local reasoning / offline mental checks, not the\n275\t  evaluator): confirm output dtypes/shapes match the tuple contract; confirm bf16 outputs are stored\n276\t  bf16 and `grad_router_weight` is f32; confirm masking on `T` tails and `K`/`N` tails; confirm the\n277\t  scatter accumulates over `K=8` slots per token.\n278\t- **Precision ablation discipline**: change one precision knob per candidate so a match-ratio change\n279\t  is attributable. Watch specifically the shared-path outputs (`grad_hidden_states`,\n280\t  `grad_shared_expert_{gate,up,down}_weight`) since the router outputs are ~0 and near-trivially pass.\n281\t- **Speed accounting**: geometric mean speedup across the five workloads is the ranking metric; record\n282\t  per-workload speedup to catch a config that wins on large `T` but regresses on small `T` (211/293).\n283\t- **Evidence logging**: for each evaluated candidate append one JSON record to `candidates.jsonl`\n284\t  (parent, source hash, hypothesis, validation, per-workload result, geomean, decision, cumulative\n285\t  eval count, skill usage). Never rewrite prior records.\n286\t- **Convergence / stop**: stop and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` when successive candidates no longer improve\n287\t  geomean meaningfully, or on hitting the eval/token budget. `final` only with operator approval.\n288\t\n289\t---\n290\t\n291\t## 9. Skill usage\n292\t- `KernelWiki` covers **Blackwell (SM100) / Hopper (SM90)** only. This target is **A800 / `sm_80`\n293\t  (Ampere)**, and the techniques (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/NVFP4/2-SM/FA4/tcgen05 warp-spec) do not apply.\n294\t  Therefore KernelWiki is **not invoked** for this task; recorded as \"not applicable (Ampere)\".\n295\t- No other external agents/tools/web are used, per isolation rules.\n296\t\n297\t## 10. Open questions / assumptions\n298\t- Assumed comparison rule `|a−b| ≤ atol + rtol·|b|` with per-tensor 98% match; if the evaluator uses a\n299\t  stricter/looser rule, the generous atols still leave large headroom on the shared path.\n300\t- Assumed the reference torch implementation is the speed baseline; our job is a faster *Triton* path,\n301\t  primarily via fusion/launch reduction rather than beating cuBLAS on isolated GEMMs.\n302\t- Assumed `torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32` state does not materially change the reference's f32\n303\t  weight-grad GEMMs relative to our Triton path — irrelevant for router (≈0) and absorbed by tolerance\n304\t  for shared weight-grads (bf16 output).\n305\t- `router_logits` input is unused by the reference `run`; we ignore it too.\n306\t- To be verified on first evaluation: installed Triton/torch versions and available `tl.dot`\n307\t  precision/`input_precision` API surface on this Ampere build (probed indirectly via a passing c001).\n308\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"36bfc9c8-ca8e-4e11-a998-a907af048eab","uuid":"71f2b65a-f6f8-4daa-bef1-45f3181d38af","timestamp":"2026-09-17T11:57:48.869Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-080_moe_complete_layer_with_shared_expert_backward/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L2/080 MoE complete layer with shared expert (backward)\n\nRun ID: `formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-080_moe_complete_layer_with_shared_expert_backward`\nTarget GPU: **NVIDIA A800, `sm_80` (Ampere)**. Triton is the required compute path.\n\n> Status: analysis only. No `plan.md` and no solution code are written in this step.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Task contract and entry point\n\nWe must expose `solution/solution.py::run(...)` with the exact signature and output\nordering of the reference in `task/definition.json`. All *computation* must be Triton;\nPyTorch may only be used for tensor metadata, allocation, dtype/stride bookkeeping, and\nkernel launch plumbing. **No** Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback is\npermitted — a failing Triton path is invalid, not something to substitute.\n\nConstant axes (fixed across all workloads):\n\n| symbol | meaning | value |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| `H`  | `hidden_size`            | 4096 |\n| `I`  | `moe_intermediate_size`  | 1408 |\n| `E`  | `n_routed_experts`       | 128  |\n| `K`  | `num_experts_per_tok`    | 8    |\n| `T`  | `batch_seq_len` (variable) | 211, 293, 384, 512, 997 (feedback set) |\n\nScalars baked into the reference: `routed_scaling_factor = 1.0`, `norm_topk_prob = True`,\n`n_group = topk_group = 1` (so `score_mask` is all ones), `eps = 1e-20`.\n\n### Inputs (dtype / shape)\n- `grad_output`  bf16 `[T,H]`\n- `hidden_states`  bf16 `[T,H]`\n- `router_weight`  bf16 `[E,H]`\n- `e_score_correction_bias`  f32 `[E]`  (all zeros in the generator, but not assumed)\n- `router_logits`  f32 `[T,E]`  (unused by the reference `run`)\n- `scores`  f32 `[T,E]`  (sigmoid outputs)\n- `topk_indices`  int64 `[T,K]`\n- `topk_weights`  f32 `[T,K]`  (already normalized to sum≈1 per row in the generator)\n- `score_mask`  f32 `[T,E]`  (all ones here)\n- `shared_expert_gate_weight`  bf16 `[I,H]`\n- `shared_expert_up_weight`  bf16 `[I,H]`\n- `shared_expert_down_weight`  bf16 `[H,I]`\n- `shared_gate_output`  bf16 `[T,I]`\n- `shared_up_output`  bf16 `[T,I]`\n- `shared_activated`  bf16 `[T,I]`\n\n### Outputs (ordered tuple)\n1. `grad_hidden_states`  bf16 `[T,H]`\n2. `grad_router_weight`  **f32** `[E,H]`\n3. `grad_shared_expert_gate_weight`  bf16 `[I,H]`\n4. `grad_shared_expert_up_weight`  bf16 `[I,H]`\n5. `grad_shared_expert_down_weight`  bf16 `[H,I]`\n\nNote the reference `run` is decorated `@torch.no_grad()` — it is a hand-written backward,\nnot autograd. We reproduce its arithmetic exactly (op-for-op semantics), not \"the correct\nmath\"; the reference contains an intentional *approximation* for the routed-expert path\n(see §4.3) that we must match, not fix.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Exact decomposition of the reference computation\n\nTwo independent paths that both accumulate into `grad_hidden_states`.\n\n### 2.1 Shared-expert path (numerically dominant)\n\nLet `go = grad_output` (`grad_shared_output` is just a clone of it).\n\n- **G1** `grad_shared_activated = go @ down_w`  → `[T,I]`.\n  `down_w` is `[H,I]`; bf16·bf16, fp32 accumulate, output bf16. Contraction `K=H=4096`.\n- **G2** `grad_shared_expert_down_weight = (go.T.f32) @ (shared_activated.f32)` → `[H,I]`,\n  cast to bf16. Contraction `K=T` (small). **Output #5.**\n- **SwiGLU backward** (elementwise, `[T,I]`):\n  - `grad_gate_silu = grad_shared_activated * shared_up_output`\n  - `grad_up_output = grad_shared_activated * silu(shared_gate_output)`   ← `grad_shared_up_output`\n  - `sig = sigmoid(gate.f32)`; `silu'(x) = sig·(1 + x·(1−sig))`\n  - `grad_gate_output = (grad_gate_silu.f32) · silu'(gate)` → bf16   ← `grad_shared_gate_output`\n- **G3** `grad_hidden_from_shared_up = grad_up_output @ up_w`  → `[T,H]` bf16. `up_w=[I,H]`, `K=I`.\n- **G4** `grad_hidden_from_shared_gate = grad_gate_output @ gate_w` → `[T,H]` bf16. `K=I`.\n- **G5** `grad_shared_expert_up_weight = (grad_up_output.T.f32) @ (hidden.f32)` → `[I,H]`→bf16.\n  `K=T`. **Output #4.**\n- **G6** `grad_shared_expert_gate_weight = (grad_gate_output.T.f32) @ (hidden.f32)` → `[I,H]`→bf16.\n  `K=T`. **Output #3.**\n- `grad_hidden_states = grad_hidden_from_shared_up + grad_hidden_from_shared_gate`.\n\n### 2.2 Router path (numerically ~zero, but must be produced)\n\n- `grad_norm_sq = rowsum(go.f32²)` → `[T,1]`; `grad_topk_weights = grad_norm_sq / K` broadcast to `[T,K]`.\n  (This is the *isotropic straight-through* approximation; see §4.3.)\n- Normalization-Jacobian (quotient rule), with `routed_scaling_factor=1`:\n  - `S = rowsum(topk_weights) + 1e-20`\n  - `sum_grad = rowsum(grad_topk_weights · topk_weights) / S`\n  - `grad_topk_before_norm = (grad_topk_weights − sum_grad) / S`   → `[T,K]`\n- **Scatter** `grad_scores[T,E] = 0; scatter_add_(dim=1, topk_indices, grad_topk_before_norm)`.\n- `grad_scores *= score_mask` (all ones ⇒ no-op, but keep general).\n- `grad_router_logits = grad_scores · scores · (1 − scores)` → `[T,E]` f32 (sigmoid derivative).\n- **G7** `grad_hidden_from_router = grad_router_logits.to(bf16) @ router_w` → `[T,H]` bf16. `K=E=128`.\n- **G8** `grad_router_weight = grad_router_logits.T @ hidden.f32` → `[E,H]` **f32**. `K=T`. **Output #2.**\n- `grad_hidden_states += grad_hidden_from_router`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Compute / cost analysis\n\nEight GEMMs plus a handful of elementwise/reduction/scatter kernels. Dominant FLOPs are the\nsix shared-path GEMMs (each ≈ `2·T·H·I`, with `H·I ≈ 5.77M`); router GEMMs use `E=128`\ninstead of `I=1408` (≈11× cheaper) and the scatter is trivial.\n\nTwo structural families of GEMM:\n- **\"Tall/skinny output\" GEMMs** with contraction `K=H` or `K=I` (G1, G3, G4, G7): output has\n  `T` rows (211–997) → short-M GEMMs.\n- **\"Outer-product\" weight-grad GEMMs** with contraction `K=T` (small): G2 `[H,I]`, G5/G6 `[I,H]`,\n  G8 `[E,H]`. Small contraction, large outputs (`[H,I]` bf16 ≈ 11.5 MB each). These are\n  latency/memory sensitive, not FLOP-bound.\n\nBecause `T` is small, every GEMM here is a *small* GEMM where kernel-launch overhead and\nmemory round-trips dominate. That is precisely the regime where a **fused Triton**\nimplementation can beat the reference's ~8 cuBLAS calls + ~6 elementwise kernels + a\nscatter, even if a single Triton GEMM is not faster than cuBLAS in isolation. The win comes\nfrom *fewer launches* and *fewer materialized intermediates*, not from out-GEMMing cuBLAS.\n\n### 3.1 Fusion opportunities (the core of the speedup thesis)\n\n- **Epilogue-fuse SwiGLU backward into G1**: G1 produces `grad_shared_activated[T,I]`; the\n  SwiGLU-backward elementwise turns it (with `shared_up_output`, `shared_gate_output`) into\n  `grad_up_output` and `grad_gate_output`. Fusing this into G1's epilogue avoids writing\n  `grad_shared_activated` and a separate elementwise pass over `[T,I]`.\n- **Concatenate the grad_hidden GEMMs G3+G4+G7** into one GEMM:\n  `grad_hidden = [grad_up_output | grad_gate_output | grad_router_logits.bf16] @ [up_w; gate_w; router_w]`\n  with contraction `K = I + I + E = 2944`. One `[T,H]` output, one accumulator, replaces three\n  GEMMs and two adds. (All three left operands are bf16, all three right operands bf16 ⇒ clean.)\n- **Concatenate the shared weight-grad GEMMs G5+G6** into one GEMM\n  `[grad_up_output | grad_gate_output].T @ hidden → [2I, H]` (contraction `K=T`), splitting the\n  `[2I,H]` output into the two `[I,H]` bf16 results. G2 is a separate `[H,I]` output (different\n  left operand `go`, different N-operand `shared_activated`) but shares `K=T`.\n- **Router path** (G8 f32, scatter, sigmoid-deriv, normalization) is cheap and numerically\n  delicate; keep it as small dedicated kernels rather than forcing it into the fused bf16 GEMMs.\n\nThese are *plan-stage* choices; the draft only enumerates the design space.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Numerical analysis and risks\n\n### 4.1 Magnitudes (from the generator's distributions)\n- `grad_output ~ N(0,1)`, `H=4096` ⇒ per-row `grad_norm_sq ≈ 4096`; `grad_topk_weights ≈ 512`.\n- Weights scaled by `0.02`; `shared_gate_output/up ~ std≈1.3`; `silu(gate) ~ O(0.5)`.\n- `grad_shared_activated ~ std≈1.3`; `grad_up_output/grad_gate_output ~ O(0.6)`.\n- `grad_hidden_states ~ O(0.4–0.6)` (dominated by the shared up+gate contributions).\n- Shared weight-grads `[I,H]/[H,I]`: contraction `K=T` of `O(0.6)·O(1)` terms ⇒ `O(√T · 0.6)`,\n  i.e. tens; cast to bf16.\n\n### 4.2 dtype-matching (must reproduce reference casts)\n- G1, G3, G4, G7 are bf16·bf16 → bf16 in the reference. Triton `tl.dot` on bf16 inputs with an\n  fp32 accumulator reproduces this (tensor-core computes exact bf16 products, fp32 accumulate),\n  then we round to bf16 on store. **Match risk: low.**\n- G2, G5, G6 upcast bf16 operands to f32 then matmul, then cast the *output* to bf16. Since\n  bf16→f32 is exact, an fp32-accumulate tensor-core GEMM of the bf16 operands yields the same\n  products; the only question is the accumulator/rounding. Given the bf16 output cast, TF32 or\n  bf16-input fp32-accumulate is more than sufficient. **Match risk: low.**\n- G8 keeps f32 output (`grad_router_weight`). Its left operand `grad_router_logits` is genuine\n  f32 (not bf16). But it is numerically ~0 (see §4.3), so precision of this GEMM is immaterial\n  to passing tolerance; still, prefer an fp32 (TF32-accumulate acceptable) path here.\n- `silu`, `sigmoid`, and the SiLU derivative are computed in f32 in the reference before the\n  bf16 cast; do the same in-kernel (compute in fp32, store bf16).\n\n### 4.3 The router-path cancellation (key insight)\nBecause `grad_topk_weights` is *isotropic* (identical value `g` across the `K` slots) and the\nnormalization Jacobian removes the common-mode component:\n`grad_before[j] = (g − g·S/S)/S ≈ 0`. With `topk_weights` already summing to ≈1 and `eps=1e-20`,\n`grad_topk_before_norm ≈ 0` up to fp rounding. Consequently `grad_router_logits ≈ 0`,\n`grad_router_weight ≈ 0`, and `grad_hidden_from_router ≈ 0`.\n\nImplications:\n- `grad_router_weight` (output #2) is a near-zero tensor; comparisons are effectively `atol`-only,\n  which is very forgiving. We must still *produce* it via the same formula (a tiny residual), but\n  precision is a non-issue.\n- The **entire measured error budget lives in the shared-expert path**. Optimization aggressiveness\n  (TF32 vs bf16 accumulate, tiling, split-K) is bounded by the shared-path outputs, not the router.\n- Risk: if we naively \"simplify\" the router path to exactly 0, the residual might differ from the\n  reference by ~1e-4, still far inside `atol`. We will nonetheless reproduce the formula faithfully\n  to avoid surprises and to be robust if a non-zero `e_score_correction_bias` ever appears.\n\n### 4.4 Tolerance interpretation\nFeedback tolerances: `max_atol ∈ {0.12, 0.13, 0.18, 0.2, 0.36}`, `max_rtol = 0.05`,\n`required_match_ratio = 0.98`. Assumed check: element passes if `|a−b| ≤ atol + rtol·|b|`, and\n≥98% of elements per tensor must pass. Given output magnitudes `O(0.5)` and these atols, bf16\nrounding (~2⁻⁸ relative ≈ 0.4%) is comfortably inside budget. This confirms we may use bf16\ntensor cores / TF32 freely on the shared path. The 2% \"free\" fraction also absorbs occasional\nlarge-relative-error elements near zero.\n\n### 4.5 Other correctness hazards\n- **`int64` topk_indices**: scatter target indices; must gather/scatter with int64 or safely cast\n  to int32 (values < 128, safe). Triton scatter via `tl.atomic_add` or a per-token serial loop over\n  `K=8`; must guard bounds and match `scatter_add_` accumulation order semantics (add is commutative\n  so order does not affect result, only fp associativity — negligible here since values ~0).\n- **Transposes / strides**: G2/G5/G6/G8 use logical transposes of the left operand. Handle via\n  stride arithmetic in the kernel (do not physically transpose). Weight tensors have specific\n  row/col-major layouts (`down_w`=[H,I], `up_w`/`gate_w`=[I,H]); tile pointers must respect these.\n- **Non-multiple `T`**: `T ∈ {211,293,384,512,997}` — 211, 293, 997 are not multiples of typical\n  block sizes; masking on the `T` dimension (both as M-dim and as K-dim in the outer-product GEMMs)\n  is mandatory. Masked K-loop tails must contribute zero.\n- **Accumulator init / additive outputs**: `grad_hidden_states` is a sum of contributions; if built\n  from a single fused concatenated GEMM the accumulator handles it, otherwise use one zero-init plus\n  in-kernel adds (avoid read-modify-write races across programs).\n- **Autotune / immutability**: Triton autotuning must be deterministic and self-contained within a\n  candidate; any config change is a new candidate ID. Prefer a small fixed config set keyed on `T`\n  buckets to avoid nondeterministic tuning across evaluations.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Constraints checklist\n- Triton-only compute; torch for allocation/metadata/launch only. No fallback of any kind.\n- Immutable sequential candidates `c001, c002, …`; new ID for any meaningful source/config/launch change.\n- Evaluate exclusively via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`; five fixed workloads = one evaluation.\n- Never run `final` without operator approval. Do not touch evaluator/dataset/controller/launcher/config.\n- Budget: ≤100 candidate evaluations; token soft/normal/absolute = 1.0M / 1.5M / 1.65M.\n- Do not run CUDA/profiler/`nvidia-smi`/the raw evaluator directly.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Triton design space\n\n### 6.1 Kernel decomposition options (from conservative → aggressive)\n- **D0 (baseline correctness)**: one Triton kernel per reference op — 8 GEMM kernels + separate\n  elementwise (SwiGLU-bwd, sigmoid-deriv), a row-reduction for `grad_norm_sq`, a scatter kernel,\n  and the normalization. Maximizes correctness confidence; minimal fusion. Establishes that a pure\n  Triton path passes and gives a speed reference vs. torch.\n- **D1 (epilogue fusion)**: fuse SwiGLU backward into G1's epilogue; fuse the sigmoid-derivative and\n  normalization into small elementwise kernels; fuse the three grad_hidden GEMMs into one\n  concatenated-K GEMM (G3+G4+G7); fuse G5+G6 into one concatenated-output GEMM.\n- **D2 (aggressive)**: additionally fuse the `grad_norm_sq` row-reduction + normalization + scatter +\n  sigmoid-deriv into a single router prologue; consider split-K for the `K=T` outer-product GEMMs\n  (G2/G5/G6) when `T` is small to keep SMs busy; persistent-kernel / grouped launch to cut overhead.\n\n### 6.2 GEMM tiling and precision\n- Short-M GEMMs (G1/G3/G4/G7): block `[BM, BN]` with `BM` covering the small `T` (e.g. 64/128), `BN`\n  along `H` or `I`; K-loop over `H`/`I`/`2944`. bf16 inputs, fp32 accumulate.\n- Outer-product GEMMs (G2/G5/G6/G8): contraction `K=T` (small, 211–997). Large outputs; tile\n  `[BM,BN]` over the output; single (or few) K-tiles. Consider split-K + atomic/second-pass reduce for\n  small `T` to improve occupancy. bf16 in / fp32 accum for G2/G5/G6; f32/TF32 for G8.\n- Precision knobs: `tl.dot(..., input_precision=\"tf32\"|\"ieee\")` and bf16 operands. Default plan: bf16\n  tensor cores with fp32 accumulate on the shared path (matches reference, fast on Ampere); reserve\n  IEEE/TF32 only where a candidate shows a match-ratio regression.\n\n### 6.3 Autotuning strategy\n- Small, fixed candidate-local config lists (block sizes, `num_warps`, `num_stages`) selected by a `T`\n  bucket, chosen to be deterministic. Ampere `num_stages` typically 3–4; `num_warps` 4–8. Avoid broad\n  autotune sweeps that could vary across evaluations or blow the token/eval budget.\n\n### 6.4 Launch/plumbing\n- Preallocate all five outputs with `torch.empty`/`zeros` (zeros only where an additive accumulation\n  requires it). Pass strides explicitly. Concatenated-K GEMMs read multiple source tensors via\n  separate base pointers within one kernel rather than a physical `torch.cat` (avoid extra copies),\n  unless a cheap `cat` is measured to be faster.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Candidate roadmap (sketch, to be formalized in plan.md)\n1. **c001** — D0 straightforward per-op Triton (correctness anchor + baseline speed).\n2. **c002** — D1 epilogue-fuse SwiGLU backward into G1; keep other GEMMs separate.\n3. **c003** — D1 concatenated grad_hidden GEMM (G3+G4+G7) and fused G5+G6.\n4. **c004+** — D2 tuning: split-K for `K=T` GEMMs, block/warp/stage tuning per `T` bucket, router\n   prologue fusion. Each measured; keep only non-regressing, correctness-passing steps.\nAdvance only when the previous candidate passes all five workloads.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Validation strategy\n- **Correctness gate**: every candidate must pass all five feedback workloads (≥98% element match at\n  the per-workload tolerance) before its speed is considered. A candidate that fails any workload is\n  invalid and is not promoted.\n- **Numerical self-checks before evaluating** (local reasoning / offline mental checks, not the\n  evaluator): confirm output dtypes/shapes match the tuple contract; confirm bf16 outputs are stored\n  bf16 and `grad_router_weight` is f32; confirm masking on `T` tails and `K`/`N` tails; confirm the\n  scatter accumulates over `K=8` slots per token.\n- **Precision ablation discipline**: change one precision knob per candidate so a match-ratio change\n  is attributable. Watch specifically the shared-path outputs (`grad_hidden_states`,\n  `grad_shared_expert_{gate,up,down}_weight`) since the router outputs are ~0 and near-trivially pass.\n- **Speed accounting**: geometric mean speedup across the five workloads is the ranking metric; record\n  per-workload speedup to catch a config that wins on large `T` but regresses on small `T` (211/293).\n- **Evidence logging**: for each evaluated candidate append one JSON record to `candidates.jsonl`\n  (parent, source hash, hypothesis, validation, per-workload result, geomean, decision, cumulative\n  eval count, skill usage). Never rewrite prior records.\n- **Convergence / stop**: stop and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` when successive candidates no longer improve\n  geomean meaningfully, or on hitting the eval/token budget. `final` only with operator approval.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Skill usage\n- `KernelWiki` covers **Blackwell (SM100) / Hopper (SM90)** only. This target is **A800 / `sm_80`\n  (Ampere)**, and the techniques (tcgen05/TMEM/CLC/NVFP4/2-SM/FA4/tcgen05 warp-spec) do not apply.\n  Therefore KernelWiki is **not invoked** for this task; recorded as \"not applicable (Ampere)\".\n- No other external agents/tools/web are used, per isolation rules.\n\n## 10. Open questions / assumptions\n- Assumed comparison rule `|a−b| ≤ atol + rtol·|b|` with per-tensor 98% match; if the evaluator uses a\n  stricter/looser rule, the generous atols still leave large headroom on the shared path.\n- Assumed the reference torch implementation is the speed baseline; our job is a faster *Triton* path,\n  primarily via fusion/launch reduction rather than beating cuBLAS on isolated GEMMs.\n- Assumed `torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32` state does not materially change the reference's f32\n  weight-grad GEMMs relative to our Triton path — irrelevant for router (≈0) and absorbed by tolerance\n  for shared weight-grads (bf16 output).\n- `router_logits` input is unused by the reference `run`; we ignore it too.\n- To be verified on first evaluation: installed Triton/torch versions and available `tl.dot`\n  precision/`input_precision` API surface on this Ampere build (probed indirectly via a passing c001).\n","numLines":308,"startLine":1,"totalLines":308}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y7DFkbDASAZGi1GM8rHNsp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t    \"name\": \"080_moe_complete_layer_with_shared_expert_backward\",\n3\t    \"hf_id\": \"zai-org/GLM-4.5-Air\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Backward pass for complete MoE layer with shared expert. Computes gradients through router (sigmoid + top-k selection), 128 routed experts with SwiGLU, and shared expert with SwiGLU. Gradients flow back through scatter-gather routing pattern.\",\n5\t    \"axes\": {\n6\t        \"batch_seq_len\": {\n7\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n8\t            \"description\": \"Batch size times sequence length (flattened)\"\n9\t        },\n10\t        \"hidden_size\": {\n11\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n12\t            \"value\": 4096,\n13\t            \"description\": null\n14\t        },\n15\t        \"moe_intermediate_size\": {\n16\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n17\t            \"value\": 1408,\n18\t            \"description\": null\n19\t        },\n20\t        \"n_routed_experts\": {\n21\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n22\t            \"value\": 128,\n23\t            \"description\": null\n24\t        },\n25\t        \"num_experts_per_tok\": {\n26\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n27\t            \"value\": 8,\n28\t            \"description\": null\n29\t        }\n30\t    },\n31\t    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": \"get_inputs\",\n32\t    \"inputs\": {\n33\t        \"grad_output\": {\n34\t            \"shape\": [\n35\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n36\t                \"hidden_size\"\n37\t            ],\n38\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n39\t            \"description\": \"Gradient from next layer\"\n40\t        },\n41\t        \"hidden_states\": {\n42\t            \"shape\": [\n43\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n44\t                \"hidden_size\"\n45\t            ],\n46\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n47\t            \"description\": \"Original input hidden states (saved from forward)\"\n48\t        },\n49\t        \"router_weight\": {\n50\t            \"shape\": [\n51\t                \"n_routed_experts\",\n52\t                \"hidden_size\"\n53\t            ],\n54\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n55\t            \"description\": \"Router linear projection weights\"\n56\t        },\n57\t        \"e_score_correction_bias\": {\n58\t            \"shape\": [\n59\t                \"n_routed_experts\"\n60\t            ],\n61\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n62\t            \"description\": \"Score correction bias for routing\"\n63\t        },\n64\t        \"router_logits\": {\n65\t            \"shape\": [\n66\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n67\t                \"n_routed_experts\"\n68\t            ],\n69\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n70\t            \"description\": \"Router logits (saved from forward)\"\n71\t        },\n72\t        \"scores\": {\n73\t            \"shape\": [\n74\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n75\t                \"n_routed_experts\"\n76\t            ],\n77\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n78\t            \"description\": \"Sigmoid scores (saved from forward)\"\n79\t        },\n80\t        \"topk_indices\": {\n81\t            \"shape\": [\n82\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n83\t                \"num_experts_per_tok\"\n84\t            ],\n85\t            \"dtype\": \"int64\",\n86\t            \"description\": \"Top-k expert indices per token\"\n87\t        },\n88\t        \"topk_weights\": {\n89\t            \"shape\": [\n90\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n91\t                \"num_experts_per_tok\"\n92\t            ],\n93\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n94\t            \"description\": \"Top-k routing weights per token\"\n95\t        },\n96\t        \"score_mask\": {\n97\t            \"shape\": [\n98\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n99\t                \"n_routed_experts\"\n100\t            ],\n101\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n102\t            \"description\": \"Group selection mask\"\n103\t        },\n104\t        \"shared_expert_gate_weight\": {\n105\t            \"shape\": [\n106\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n107\t                \"hidden_size\"\n108\t            ],\n109\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n110\t            \"description\": \"Shared expert gate projection\"\n111\t        },\n112\t        \"shared_expert_up_weight\": {\n113\t            \"shape\": [\n114\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n115\t                \"hidden_size\"\n116\t            ],\n117\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n118\t            \"description\": \"Shared expert up projection\"\n119\t        },\n120\t        \"shared_expert_down_weight\": {\n121\t            \"shape\": [\n122\t                \"hidden_size\",\n123\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n124\t            ],\n125\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n126\t            \"description\": \"Shared expert down projection\"\n127\t        },\n128\t        \"shared_gate_output\": {\n129\t            \"shape\": [\n130\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n131\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n132\t            ],\n133\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n134\t            \"description\": \"Shared expert gate output (saved from forward)\"\n135\t        },\n136\t        \"shared_up_output\": {\n137\t            \"shape\": [\n138\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n139\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n140\t            ],\n141\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n142\t            \"description\": \"Shared expert up output (saved from forward)\"\n143\t        },\n144\t        \"shared_activated\": {\n145\t            \"shape\": [\n146\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n147\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n148\t            ],\n149\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n150\t            \"description\": \"Shared expert activated output (saved from forward)\"\n151\t        }\n152\t    },\n153\t    \"outputs\": {\n154\t        \"grad_hidden_states\": {\n155\t            \"shape\": [\n156\t                \"batch_seq_len\",\n157\t                \"hidden_size\"\n158\t            ],\n159\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n160\t            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. input hidden states\"\n161\t        },\n162\t        \"grad_router_weight\": {\n163\t            \"shape\": [\n164\t                \"n_routed_experts\",\n165\t                \"hidden_size\"\n166\t            ],\n167\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n168\t            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. router weights\"\n169\t        },\n170\t        \"grad_shared_expert_gate_weight\": {\n171\t            \"shape\": [\n172\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n173\t                \"hidden_size\"\n174\t            ],\n175\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n176\t            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. shared expert gate weights\"\n177\t        },\n178\t        \"grad_shared_expert_up_weight\": {\n179\t            \"shape\": [\n180\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n181\t                \"hidden_size\"\n182\t            ],\n183\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n184\t            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. shared expert up weights\"\n185\t        },\n186\t        \"grad_shared_expert_down_weight\": {\n187\t            \"shape\": [\n188\t                \"hidden_size\",\n189\t                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n190\t            ],\n191\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n192\t            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. shared expert down weights\"\n193\t        }\n194\t    },\n195\t    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\n\\ndef get_inputs(axes_and_scalars: dict, device: torch.device) -> dict:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Generate inputs for backward pass testing.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    batch_seq_len = axes_and_scalars[\\\"batch_seq_len\\\"]\\n    hidden_size = 4096\\n    moe_intermediate_size = 1408\\n    n_routed_experts = 128\\n    num_experts_per_tok = 8\\n    routed_scaling_factor = 1.0\\n    \\n    # Gradient from next layer\\n    grad_output = torch.randn(batch_seq_len, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    \\n    # Original hidden states\\n    hidden_states = torch.randn(batch_seq_len, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    \\n    # Router weights\\n    router_weight = torch.randn(n_routed_experts, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) * 0.02\\n    \\n    # Score correction bias\\n    e_score_correction_bias = torch.zeros(n_routed_experts, dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\\n    \\n    # Compute router logits and scores for realistic saved tensors\\n    router_logits = F.linear(hidden_states.to(torch.float32), router_weight.to(torch.float32))\\n    scores = torch.sigmoid(router_logits)\\n    \\n    # Compute top-k selection\\n    scores_for_choice = scores + e_score_correction_bias.unsqueeze(0)\\n    topk_weights, topk_indices = torch.topk(scores_for_choice, k=num_experts_per_tok, dim=-1, sorted=False)\\n    \\n    # Normalize weights\\n    denominator = topk_weights.sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 1e-20\\n    topk_weights = (topk_weights / denominator) * routed_scaling_factor\\n    \\n    # Score mask (all ones for n_group=1, topk_group=1)\\n    score_mask = torch.ones(batch_seq_len, n_routed_experts, dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\\n    \\n    # Shared expert weights\\n    shared_expert_gate_weight = torch.randn(moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) * 0.02\\n    shared_expert_up_weight = torch.randn(moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) * 0.02\\n    shared_expert_down_weight = torch.randn(hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) * 0.02\\n    \\n    # Compute shared expert forward pass for saved tensors\\n    shared_gate_output = F.linear(hidden_states, shared_expert_gate_weight)\\n    shared_up_output = F.linear(hidden_states, shared_expert_up_weight)\\n    shared_activated = F.silu(shared_gate_output) * shared_up_output\\n    \\n    return {\\n        \\\"grad_output\\\": grad_output,\\n        \\\"hidden_states\\\": hidden_states,\\n        \\\"router_weight\\\": router_weight,\\n        \\\"e_score_correction_bias\\\": e_score_correction_bias,\\n        \\\"router_logits\\\": router_logits,\\n        \\\"scores\\\": scores,\\n        \\\"topk_indices\\\": topk_indices,\\n        \\\"topk_weights\\\": topk_weights,\\n        \\\"score_mask\\\": score_mask,\\n        \\\"shared_expert_gate_weight\\\": shared_expert_gate_weight,\\n        \\\"shared_expert_up_weight\\\": shared_expert_up_weight,\\n        \\\"shared_expert_down_weight\\\": shared_expert_down_weight,\\n        \\\"shared_gate_output\\\": shared_gate_output,\\n        \\\"shared_up_output\\\": shared_up_output,\\n        \\\"shared_activated\\\": shared_activated,\\n    }\\n\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    grad_output: torch.Tensor,\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    router_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    e_score_correction_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    router_logits: torch.Tensor,\\n    scores: torch.Tensor,\\n    topk_indices: torch.Tensor,\\n    topk_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    score_mask: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_expert_gate_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_expert_up_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_expert_down_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_gate_output: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_up_output: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_activated: torch.Tensor,\\n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Backward pass for MoE layer with shared expert.\\n    \\n    Computes gradients for:\\n    - hidden_states (input)\\n    - router_weight\\n    - shared_expert_gate_weight, shared_expert_up_weight, shared_expert_down_weight\\n    \\n    Note: Routed expert weight gradients are omitted for simplicity as they would\\n    require passing all 128*3 weight tensors and their saved activations.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    batch_seq_len = hidden_states.shape[0]\\n    hidden_size = hidden_states.shape[1]\\n    n_routed_experts = 128\\n    norm_topk_prob = True\\n    routed_scaling_factor = 1.0\\n    \\n    # Initialize gradients\\n    grad_hidden_states = torch.zeros_like(hidden_states)\\n    \\n    # Gradient flows through addition: split to routed and shared paths\\n    grad_shared_output = grad_output.clone()\\n    \\n    # ===== Backward through shared expert =====\\n    # Gradient through shared_expert_down: output = down(activated)\\n    # down_weight shape: [hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size]\\n    # grad_shared_output shape: [batch_seq_len, hidden_size]\\n    # grad_shared_activated = grad_shared_output @ down_weight (need transpose for backward)\\n    grad_shared_activated = grad_shared_output @ shared_expert_down_weight\\n    \\n    # grad_shared_expert_down_weight = grad_shared_output.T @ shared_activated\\n    # Shape: [hidden_size, batch_seq_len] @ [batch_seq_len, moe_intermediate_size] = [hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size]\\n    grad_shared_expert_down_weight = grad_shared_output.t().to(torch.float32) @ shared_activated.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_shared_expert_down_weight = grad_shared_expert_down_weight.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    # Gradient through SwiGLU: activated = silu(gate) * up\\n    grad_shared_gate_silu = grad_shared_activated * shared_up_output\\n    grad_shared_up_output = grad_shared_activated * F.silu(shared_gate_output)\\n    \\n    # Gradient through silu: silu(x) = x * sigmoid(x)\\n    sigmoid_gate = torch.sigmoid(shared_gate_output.to(torch.float32))\\n    shared_gate_float = shared_gate_output.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_shared_gate_output = grad_shared_gate_silu.to(torch.float32) * (\\n        sigmoid_gate * (1 + shared_gate_float * (1 - sigmoid_gate))\\n    )\\n    grad_shared_gate_output = grad_shared_gate_output.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    # Gradient through shared_expert_up and shared_expert_gate\\n    # up_weight shape: [moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size]\\n    # grad_shared_up_output shape: [batch_seq_len, moe_intermediate_size]\\n    # grad_hidden_from_shared_up = grad_shared_up_output @ up_weight\\n    grad_hidden_from_shared_up = grad_shared_up_output @ shared_expert_up_weight\\n    grad_hidden_from_shared_gate = grad_shared_gate_output @ shared_expert_gate_weight\\n    \\n    # grad_shared_expert_up_weight = grad_shared_up_output.T @ hidden_states\\n    # Shape: [moe_intermediate_size, batch_seq_len] @ [batch_seq_len, hidden_size] = [moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size]\\n    grad_shared_expert_up_weight = grad_shared_up_output.t().to(torch.float32) @ hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_shared_expert_up_weight = grad_shared_expert_up_weight.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    grad_shared_expert_gate_weight = grad_shared_gate_output.t().to(torch.float32) @ hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_shared_expert_gate_weight = grad_shared_expert_gate_weight.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    grad_hidden_states = grad_hidden_states + grad_hidden_from_shared_up + grad_hidden_from_shared_gate\\n    \\n    # ===== Backward through routing =====\\n    # Routed expert output: y_routed = sum_k(w_norm_k * expert_k(x))\\n    # grad_topk_weights_norm[token, k] = dot(grad_output[token], expert_k_output[token])\\n    # Since routed expert outputs are not available as saved tensors (would require\\n    # 128*3 weight matrices and activations), we approximate using grad_output directly.\\n    # The routed contribution per token is: y_routed = sum_k(w_k * e_k), so\\n    # grad_w_k = <grad_output, e_k>. Without e_k, we use a straight-through estimator:\\n    # grad_w_k \\u2248 ||grad_output||^2 / num_experts_per_tok as an isotropic approximation.\\n    num_experts_per_tok = topk_weights.shape[-1]\\n\\n    # Approximate grad_topk_weights using the norm of grad_output as a proxy\\n    # This captures the magnitude of the gradient signal flowing through routing\\n    grad_output_f32 = grad_output.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_norm_sq = (grad_output_f32 * grad_output_f32).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)  # [batch_seq_len, 1]\\n    grad_topk_weights = grad_norm_sq.expand_as(topk_weights) / num_experts_per_tok\\n\\n    # Gradient through routing weight normalization and scaling\\n    if norm_topk_prob:\\n        # Gradient through normalization: w_norm = w / sum(w) * routed_scaling_factor\\n        topk_weights_unnorm = topk_weights / routed_scaling_factor\\n        denominator = topk_weights_unnorm.sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 1e-20\\n        grad_topk_weights_unnorm = grad_topk_weights / routed_scaling_factor\\n\\n        # Quotient rule: d/dw_i (w_i / S) = (S - w_i) / S^2\\n        sum_grad = (grad_topk_weights_unnorm * topk_weights_unnorm).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True) / denominator\\n        grad_topk_weights_before_norm = (grad_topk_weights_unnorm - sum_grad) / denominator\\n    else:\\n        grad_topk_weights_before_norm = grad_topk_weights / routed_scaling_factor\\n\\n    # Gradient through top-k selection (sparse gradient)\\n    grad_scores_for_choice = torch.zeros(batch_seq_len, n_routed_experts, dtype=torch.float32, device=hidden_states.device)\\n    grad_scores_for_choice.scatter_add_(\\n        1,\\n        topk_indices,\\n        grad_topk_weights_before_norm\\n    )\\n\\n    # Gradient through masking (only selected groups receive gradient)\\n    grad_scores_for_choice = grad_scores_for_choice * score_mask\\n\\n    # Gradient through score correction (bias is non-trainable, so only propagate to scores)\\n    grad_scores = grad_scores_for_choice\\n\\n    # Gradient through sigmoid: d/dx sigmoid(x) = sigmoid(x) * (1 - sigmoid(x))\\n    grad_router_logits = grad_scores * scores * (1 - scores)\\n\\n    # Gradient through router linear projection\\n    # router_weight shape: [n_routed_experts, hidden_size]\\n    # grad_router_logits shape: [batch_seq_len, n_routed_experts]\\n    grad_hidden_from_router = grad_router_logits.to(torch.bfloat16) @ router_weight\\n\\n    # grad_router_weight = grad_router_logits.T @ hidden_states\\n    grad_router_weight = grad_router_logits.t() @ hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n\\n    grad_hidden_states = grad_hidden_states + grad_hidden_from_router\\n    \\n    return (\\n        grad_hidden_states,\\n        grad_router_weight,\\n        grad_shared_expert_gate_weight,\\n        grad_shared_expert_up_weight,\\n        grad_shared_expert_down_weight,\\n    )\\n\"\n196\t}\n197\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"36bfc9c8-ca8e-4e11-a998-a907af048eab","uuid":"bf0e72f0-c44f-41fd-a661-67d35647d6c1","timestamp":"2026-09-17T11:57:49.232Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-080_moe_complete_layer_with_shared_expert_backward/task/definition.json","content":"{\n    \"name\": \"080_moe_complete_layer_with_shared_expert_backward\",\n    \"hf_id\": \"zai-org/GLM-4.5-Air\",\n    \"description\": \"Backward pass for complete MoE layer with shared expert. Computes gradients through router (sigmoid + top-k selection), 128 routed experts with SwiGLU, and shared expert with SwiGLU. Gradients flow back through scatter-gather routing pattern.\",\n    \"axes\": {\n        \"batch_seq_len\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Batch size times sequence length (flattened)\"\n        },\n        \"hidden_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 4096,\n            \"description\": null\n        },\n        \"moe_intermediate_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 1408,\n            \"description\": null\n        },\n        \"n_routed_experts\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 128,\n            \"description\": null\n        },\n        \"num_experts_per_tok\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 8,\n            \"description\": null\n        }\n    },\n    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": \"get_inputs\",\n    \"inputs\": {\n        \"grad_output\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Gradient from next layer\"\n        },\n        \"hidden_states\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Original input hidden states (saved from forward)\"\n        },\n        \"router_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"n_routed_experts\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Router linear projection weights\"\n        },\n        \"e_score_correction_bias\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"n_routed_experts\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Score correction bias for routing\"\n        },\n        \"router_logits\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"n_routed_experts\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Router logits (saved from forward)\"\n        },\n        \"scores\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"n_routed_experts\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Sigmoid scores (saved from forward)\"\n        },\n        \"topk_indices\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"num_experts_per_tok\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"int64\",\n            \"description\": \"Top-k expert indices per token\"\n        },\n        \"topk_weights\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"num_experts_per_tok\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Top-k routing weights per token\"\n        },\n        \"score_mask\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"n_routed_experts\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Group selection mask\"\n        },\n        \"shared_expert_gate_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Shared expert gate projection\"\n        },\n        \"shared_expert_up_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Shared expert up projection\"\n        },\n        \"shared_expert_down_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size\",\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Shared expert down projection\"\n        },\n        \"shared_gate_output\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Shared expert gate output (saved from forward)\"\n        },\n        \"shared_up_output\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Shared expert up output (saved from forward)\"\n        },\n        \"shared_activated\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Shared expert activated output (saved from forward)\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"outputs\": {\n        \"grad_hidden_states\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. input hidden states\"\n        },\n        \"grad_router_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"n_routed_experts\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. router weights\"\n        },\n        \"grad_shared_expert_gate_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. shared expert gate weights\"\n        },\n        \"grad_shared_expert_up_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\",\n                \"hidden_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. shared expert up weights\"\n        },\n        \"grad_shared_expert_down_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_size\",\n                \"moe_intermediate_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Gradient w.r.t. shared expert down weights\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\n\\ndef get_inputs(axes_and_scalars: dict, device: torch.device) -> dict:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Generate inputs for backward pass testing.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    batch_seq_len = axes_and_scalars[\\\"batch_seq_len\\\"]\\n    hidden_size = 4096\\n    moe_intermediate_size = 1408\\n    n_routed_experts = 128\\n    num_experts_per_tok = 8\\n    routed_scaling_factor = 1.0\\n    \\n    # Gradient from next layer\\n    grad_output = torch.randn(batch_seq_len, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    \\n    # Original hidden states\\n    hidden_states = torch.randn(batch_seq_len, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device)\\n    \\n    # Router weights\\n    router_weight = torch.randn(n_routed_experts, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) * 0.02\\n    \\n    # Score correction bias\\n    e_score_correction_bias = torch.zeros(n_routed_experts, dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\\n    \\n    # Compute router logits and scores for realistic saved tensors\\n    router_logits = F.linear(hidden_states.to(torch.float32), router_weight.to(torch.float32))\\n    scores = torch.sigmoid(router_logits)\\n    \\n    # Compute top-k selection\\n    scores_for_choice = scores + e_score_correction_bias.unsqueeze(0)\\n    topk_weights, topk_indices = torch.topk(scores_for_choice, k=num_experts_per_tok, dim=-1, sorted=False)\\n    \\n    # Normalize weights\\n    denominator = topk_weights.sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 1e-20\\n    topk_weights = (topk_weights / denominator) * routed_scaling_factor\\n    \\n    # Score mask (all ones for n_group=1, topk_group=1)\\n    score_mask = torch.ones(batch_seq_len, n_routed_experts, dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\\n    \\n    # Shared expert weights\\n    shared_expert_gate_weight = torch.randn(moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) * 0.02\\n    shared_expert_up_weight = torch.randn(moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) * 0.02\\n    shared_expert_down_weight = torch.randn(hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device=device) * 0.02\\n    \\n    # Compute shared expert forward pass for saved tensors\\n    shared_gate_output = F.linear(hidden_states, shared_expert_gate_weight)\\n    shared_up_output = F.linear(hidden_states, shared_expert_up_weight)\\n    shared_activated = F.silu(shared_gate_output) * shared_up_output\\n    \\n    return {\\n        \\\"grad_output\\\": grad_output,\\n        \\\"hidden_states\\\": hidden_states,\\n        \\\"router_weight\\\": router_weight,\\n        \\\"e_score_correction_bias\\\": e_score_correction_bias,\\n        \\\"router_logits\\\": router_logits,\\n        \\\"scores\\\": scores,\\n        \\\"topk_indices\\\": topk_indices,\\n        \\\"topk_weights\\\": topk_weights,\\n        \\\"score_mask\\\": score_mask,\\n        \\\"shared_expert_gate_weight\\\": shared_expert_gate_weight,\\n        \\\"shared_expert_up_weight\\\": shared_expert_up_weight,\\n        \\\"shared_expert_down_weight\\\": shared_expert_down_weight,\\n        \\\"shared_gate_output\\\": shared_gate_output,\\n        \\\"shared_up_output\\\": shared_up_output,\\n        \\\"shared_activated\\\": shared_activated,\\n    }\\n\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    grad_output: torch.Tensor,\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    router_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    e_score_correction_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    router_logits: torch.Tensor,\\n    scores: torch.Tensor,\\n    topk_indices: torch.Tensor,\\n    topk_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    score_mask: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_expert_gate_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_expert_up_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_expert_down_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_gate_output: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_up_output: torch.Tensor,\\n    shared_activated: torch.Tensor,\\n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Backward pass for MoE layer with shared expert.\\n    \\n    Computes gradients for:\\n    - hidden_states (input)\\n    - router_weight\\n    - shared_expert_gate_weight, shared_expert_up_weight, shared_expert_down_weight\\n    \\n    Note: Routed expert weight gradients are omitted for simplicity as they would\\n    require passing all 128*3 weight tensors and their saved activations.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    batch_seq_len = hidden_states.shape[0]\\n    hidden_size = hidden_states.shape[1]\\n    n_routed_experts = 128\\n    norm_topk_prob = True\\n    routed_scaling_factor = 1.0\\n    \\n    # Initialize gradients\\n    grad_hidden_states = torch.zeros_like(hidden_states)\\n    \\n    # Gradient flows through addition: split to routed and shared paths\\n    grad_shared_output = grad_output.clone()\\n    \\n    # ===== Backward through shared expert =====\\n    # Gradient through shared_expert_down: output = down(activated)\\n    # down_weight shape: [hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size]\\n    # grad_shared_output shape: [batch_seq_len, hidden_size]\\n    # grad_shared_activated = grad_shared_output @ down_weight (need transpose for backward)\\n    grad_shared_activated = grad_shared_output @ shared_expert_down_weight\\n    \\n    # grad_shared_expert_down_weight = grad_shared_output.T @ shared_activated\\n    # Shape: [hidden_size, batch_seq_len] @ [batch_seq_len, moe_intermediate_size] = [hidden_size, moe_intermediate_size]\\n    grad_shared_expert_down_weight = grad_shared_output.t().to(torch.float32) @ shared_activated.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_shared_expert_down_weight = grad_shared_expert_down_weight.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    # Gradient through SwiGLU: activated = silu(gate) * up\\n    grad_shared_gate_silu = grad_shared_activated * shared_up_output\\n    grad_shared_up_output = grad_shared_activated * F.silu(shared_gate_output)\\n    \\n    # Gradient through silu: silu(x) = x * sigmoid(x)\\n    sigmoid_gate = torch.sigmoid(shared_gate_output.to(torch.float32))\\n    shared_gate_float = shared_gate_output.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_shared_gate_output = grad_shared_gate_silu.to(torch.float32) * (\\n        sigmoid_gate * (1 + shared_gate_float * (1 - sigmoid_gate))\\n    )\\n    grad_shared_gate_output = grad_shared_gate_output.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    # Gradient through shared_expert_up and shared_expert_gate\\n    # up_weight shape: [moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size]\\n    # grad_shared_up_output shape: [batch_seq_len, moe_intermediate_size]\\n    # grad_hidden_from_shared_up = grad_shared_up_output @ up_weight\\n    grad_hidden_from_shared_up = grad_shared_up_output @ shared_expert_up_weight\\n    grad_hidden_from_shared_gate = grad_shared_gate_output @ shared_expert_gate_weight\\n    \\n    # grad_shared_expert_up_weight = grad_shared_up_output.T @ hidden_states\\n    # Shape: [moe_intermediate_size, batch_seq_len] @ [batch_seq_len, hidden_size] = [moe_intermediate_size, hidden_size]\\n    grad_shared_expert_up_weight = grad_shared_up_output.t().to(torch.float32) @ hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_shared_expert_up_weight = grad_shared_expert_up_weight.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    grad_shared_expert_gate_weight = grad_shared_gate_output.t().to(torch.float32) @ hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_shared_expert_gate_weight = grad_shared_expert_gate_weight.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    grad_hidden_states = grad_hidden_states + grad_hidden_from_shared_up + grad_hidden_from_shared_gate\\n    \\n    # ===== Backward through routing =====\\n    # Routed expert output: y_routed = sum_k(w_norm_k * expert_k(x))\\n    # grad_topk_weights_norm[token, k] = dot(grad_output[token], expert_k_output[token])\\n    # Since routed expert outputs are not available as saved tensors (would require\\n    # 128*3 weight matrices and activations), we approximate using grad_output directly.\\n    # The routed contribution per token is: y_routed = sum_k(w_k * e_k), so\\n    # grad_w_k = <grad_output, e_k>. Without e_k, we use a straight-through estimator:\\n    # grad_w_k \\u2248 ||grad_output||^2 / num_experts_per_tok as an isotropic approximation.\\n    num_experts_per_tok = topk_weights.shape[-1]\\n\\n    # Approximate grad_topk_weights using the norm of grad_output as a proxy\\n    # This captures the magnitude of the gradient signal flowing through routing\\n    grad_output_f32 = grad_output.to(torch.float32)\\n    grad_norm_sq = (grad_output_f32 * grad_output_f32).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)  # [batch_seq_len, 1]\\n    grad_topk_weights = grad_norm_sq.expand_as(topk_weights) / num_experts_per_tok\\n\\n    # Gradient through routing weight normalization and scaling\\n    if norm_topk_prob:\\n        # Gradient through normalization: w_norm = w / sum(w) * routed_scaling_factor\\n        topk_weights_unnorm = topk_weights / routed_scaling_factor\\n        denominator = topk_weights_unnorm.sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 1e-20\\n        grad_topk_weights_unnorm = grad_topk_weights / routed_scaling_factor\\n\\n        # Quotient rule: d/dw_i (w_i / S) = (S - w_i) / S^2\\n        sum_grad = (grad_topk_weights_unnorm * topk_weights_unnorm).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True) / denominator\\n        grad_topk_weights_before_norm = (grad_topk_weights_unnorm - sum_grad) / denominator\\n    else:\\n        grad_topk_weights_before_norm = grad_topk_weights / routed_scaling_factor\\n\\n    # Gradient through top-k selection (sparse gradient)\\n    grad_scores_for_choice = torch.zeros(batch_seq_len, n_routed_experts, dtype=torch.float32, device=hidden_states.device)\\n    grad_scores_for_choice.scatter_add_(\\n        1,\\n        topk_indices,\\n        grad_topk_weights_before_norm\\n    )\\n\\n    # Gradient through masking (only selected groups receive gradient)\\n    grad_scores_for_choice = grad_scores_for_choice * score_mask\\n\\n    # Gradient through score correction (bias is non-trainable, so only propagate to scores)\\n    grad_scores = grad_scores_for_choice\\n\\n    # Gradient through sigmoid: d/dx sigmoid(x) = sigmoid(x) * (1 - sigmoid(x))\\n    grad_router_logits = grad_scores * scores * (1 - scores)\\n\\n    # Gradient through router linear projection\\n    # router_weight shape: [n_routed_experts, hidden_size]\\n    # grad_router_logits shape: [batch_seq_len, n_routed_experts]\\n    grad_hidden_from_router = grad_router_logits.to(torch.bfloat16) @ router_weight\\n\\n    # grad_router_weight = grad_router_logits.T @ hidden_states\\n    grad_router_weight = grad_router_logits.t() @ hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n\\n    grad_hidden_states = grad_hidden_states + grad_hidden_from_router\\n    \\n    return (\\n        grad_hidden_states,\\n        grad_router_weight,\\n        grad_shared_expert_gate_weight,\\n        grad_shared_expert_up_weight,\\n        grad_shared_expert_down_weight,\\n    )\\n\"\n}\n","numLines":197,"startLine":1,"totalLines":197}}}
